{
  "concept": "The image formed by a spherical mirror is located exactly where the principal rays (or their backward extensions) intersect, so a concave mirror can produce a real inverted image when rays truly converge or a virtual upright image when the object is inside the focal point, while a convex mirror always yields a virtual upright image because its reflected rays only ever diverge.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "horizontal optical axis line",
    "spherical mirror drawn as a pale blue curved arc on the right (concave opening left in panel a, convex bulging left in panel b)",
    "vertex point V where the arc meets the axis, labeled V",
    "focal point F on the axis, labeled F (in front of mirror for concave, behind for convex)",
    "center of curvature C on the axis, labeled C (in front for concave, behind for convex)",
    "red upward object arrow with base P on the axis and tip Q, labeled Object",
    "red image arrow with base P' on the axis and tip Q', labeled Image (downward/inverted for concave real case, upward/upright for virtual cases)",
    "ray 1 (blue): from Q parallel to axis to mirror, reflected through F",
    "ray 2 (red): from Q through F to mirror, reflected parallel to axis",
    "ray 3 (purple): from Q toward C to mirror, reflected back on itself, with a small right-angle square at the mirror",
    "ray 4 (green): from Q to vertex V, reflected symmetrically across the axis",
    "number labels 1, 2, 3, 4 beside each ray",
    "dashed backward extensions of the reflected rays meeting at Q' behind the mirror (convex case and concave virtual case)",
    "small drawn eye icon at lower left looking toward the mirror",
    "panel captions (a) and (b)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "mirrorType",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Mirror type",
      "options": ["Concave", "Convex"],
      "default": "Concave",
      "teaches": "Switching mirror type flips where F and C sit and whether the reflected rays genuinely converge in front of the mirror (real image, concave) or spread apart so that only their dashed backward extensions meet behind it (virtual image, convex)"
    },
    {
      "id": "objectDistance",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Object distance from mirror",
      "range": [0.3, 3.5, 0.1],
      "default": 2.5,
      "teaches": "Dragging the object along the axis moves, resizes, and flips the image: for a concave mirror the image is real and inverted when the object is outside F but becomes virtual, upright and magnified once the object crosses inside F; for a convex mirror the image stays virtual, upright and reduced at every position"
    },
    {
      "id": "rayHighlight",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Selecting one numbered ray isolates its single construction rule (1: parallel in, through F out; 2: through F in, parallel out; 3: through C, returns on itself; 4: hits V, reflects symmetrically), so each rule can be read off the busy crossing diagram one at a time"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Concave mirror, object beyond C",
      "narration": "With the object far from a concave mirror, all four color-coded rays leave the tip Q and truly cross at Q' in front of the mirror, forming a real, inverted, reduced image between C and F.",
      "state": { "mirrorType": "Concave", "objectDistance": 2.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Isolate one ray rule",
      "narration": "Click ray 1 (blue): it travels parallel to the axis and reflects straight through F. Clicking each numbered ray in turn reveals the one geometric rule behind it, building up the construction ray by ray.",
      "state": { "mirrorType": "Concave", "objectDistance": 2.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the object inside F",
      "narration": "Drag the slider so the object sits between F and the mirror: the reflected rays now diverge, and only their dashed backward extensions meet behind the mirror, producing a virtual, upright, magnified image.",
      "state": { "mirrorType": "Concave", "objectDistance": 0.6 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Convex mirror always virtual",
      "narration": "Toggle to the convex mirror: F and C move behind the arc, the reflected rays always spread apart, and their dashed extensions meet at an upright, reduced virtual image behind the mirror no matter where the object is placed.",
      "state": { "mirrorType": "Convex", "objectDistance": 2.5 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "An image sits exactly where the principal rays intersect: when the rays themselves cross in front of the mirror the image is real and inverted, but when only their backward extensions cross behind the mirror the image is virtual and upright — concave mirrors can do both depending on object position, convex mirrors only the latter.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Lay out a single mirror panel at a time (per the toggle), not both stacked. Draw the optical axis horizontally with V at the mirror, then F and C along it (in front for concave, behind for convex). Object is a bold red upward arrow on the left labeled Object with base P and tip Q; image is a red arrow at P'/Q' labeled Image, drawn downward when real/inverted and upward when virtual/upright, with dashed segments for virtual images. Color the four rays consistently: ray 1 blue, ray 2 red, ray 3 purple, ray 4 green, each with its incident and reflected (or dashed extended) segment and a small number label; add a tiny right-angle square where ray 3 meets the mirror and a simple eye glyph at lower left. The mirror is a thin pale-blue arc. On slider change, recompute all four rays and the image arrow from the mirror equation so the convergence/divergence and the image's position, size and orientation update live; on ray click, dim the other three rays and brighten the selected one end to end."
}

